WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.170
If you're one of the millions of people who
saw Avengers: Infinity War on opening weekend,

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.780
chances are you've got some questions about
what you've just watched and what's going

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.920
to happen next.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.619
But more than anything, you probably want
to know about that one moment no one saw coming.

00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:15.980
You know the one...

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.410
The good news is if you're ready to dive into
the nitty gritty of the movie and its many

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.100
connections to the comics, there's no reason
to wait any longer.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.100
Let's get to it.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.720
Do we even need to say "spoilers ahead"?

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.420
"Ready?"

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:28.780
"Let's go."

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:33.460
As we see in Infinity War, Thanos forces Gamora
to take him to the Soul Stone after she's

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.210
forced to reveal its location on the planet
Vormir.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.820
There, the pair find that the keeper of the
stone is our old pal the Red Skull, who seemed

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.660
to meet his demise when he was sucked into
the Tesseract itself, a.k.a.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.430
The Space Stone, and then shot into outer
space in the first Captain America movie.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.199
He might look a little different when he shows
up in Infinity War, of course, since Hugo

00:00:53.199 --> 00:00:57.559
Weaving didn't return to play the character
and was instead portrayed by The Walking Dead's

00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:01.160
Ross Marquand, but it's still definitely the
Skull.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.579
So what's he doing all the way out on Vormir?

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:07.770
Red Skull explains that he was cast out by
the Space Stone and imprisoned on Vormir,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.600
doomed to lead others to the Soul Stone without
ever being able to use it himself.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.119
In the comics, of course, Red Skull's relationship
with the Infinity Stones isn't really a thing,

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:20.619
but he does have a lot of history with the
Cosmic Cube, an item with similar powers to

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:22.060
the MCU's Tesseract.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.640
However, the Infinity Stones are not related
to the comics' Cosmic Cube.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.270
Say that five times fast.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.130
Red Skull is one of Captain America's most
frequent comic book foes.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.979
And although the hero has vanquished him time
and time again, he uses the Cosmic Cube to

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:41.020
come back from the dead, transform into and
possess people, and travel through time.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.530
But in the MCU, the Space Stone was his jam.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.610
And now we know that he isn't even connected
to that anymore, now he just guards the Soul

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:52.700
Stone, the only one of the six Infinity Stones
that uses its owner, rather than letting its

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.290
owner use it.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:55.380
So what's it all mean?

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.399
Why would we see the Red Skull in this scene
at all?

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:00.710
Well… it may be foreshadowing.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.390
You know that there are a lot of people who
kick the bucket in the end, thanks to Thanos'

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.039
supreme snap.

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:10.160
Some of those people, including Spider-Man,
Doctor Strange, and Black Panther, are expected

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.990
to have more than just one stand-alone project
on the MCU film slate, so mourning those losses

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:17.050
seem premature, to say the least.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.600
Meanwhile, the Soul Stone in the comics tends
to capture its wielder and contain the souls

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.860
of others within it, in a place called Soul
World.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.610
So it's possible that the Red Skull wasn't
trapped on Vormir to guard the stone.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.690
Instead, he may be one of the stone's prisoners.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.290
After all, the Red Skull's been there for
decades.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.160
He should be dead, but the Soul Stone has
kept him alive this whole time.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.110
So maybe some of the other characters who
seemed to die in Infinity War have been kept

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.530
alive by the Soul Stone too...

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:50.020
This could potentially answer the question
of how some of the many superheroes who bit

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.530
the dust in the film could be brought back
in time for the fourth movie.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:58.130
Could those who were snapped out of existence
all be residing in the Soul World now?

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:02.670
There are other ways the fourth Avengers film
could bring back those lost heroes, of course,

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:07.980
especially since the Time Stone was proven
to be able to do exactly that by Thanos himself.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.400
But if the Russo brothers are looking to surprise
some folks with a more unexpected resurrection,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.730
well, the Red Skull's guardianship of the
Soul Stone might've just laid the groundwork

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:18.580
for exactly that.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:19.900
"Wow."

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.020
Thanks for watching!

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.420
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.099
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

